      "title": "Contracts vs Partial Implementations",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 0,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Contracts vs Partial Implementations",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Two kinds of abstract type",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "==Interface== = pure contract: method signatures, **no state**: `interface Shape { double area(); }`",
                "==Abstract class== = partial implementation: shared fields and concrete methods, plus abstract methods to fill in"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "Interface",
                "Abstract class"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "State",
                  "none",
                  "{green:fields allowed}"
                ],
                [
                  "Methods",
                  "signatures only",
                  "concrete + abstract"
                ],
                [
                  "Models",
                  "{blue:can do}",
                  "{purple:is a}"
                ]
              ],
              "highlightRow": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. In Python and on instantiation",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "No `interface` keyword: use `abc.ABC` with `@abstractmethod`",
                "Or `typing.Protocol` for a ==structural== (duck-typed) contract",
                "Neither can be instantiated; unfilled abstract methods in Python raise ==TypeError==",
                "Java rejects it at **compile time** instead"
              ],
              "style": "check"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "python",
              "lines": [
                "from abc import ABC, abstractmethod",
                "class Shape(ABC):",
                "    @abstractmethod",
                "    def area(self): ...",
                "try:",
                "    Shape()",
                "except TypeError:",
                "    print(\"TypeError\")"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "output",
              "lines": [
                "TypeError"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Mental model",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Pick by the question",
              "text": "Interface = **can do** (capability). Abstract class = **is a** (shared base with common code)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Multiple Interface Implementation",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 1,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Multiple Interface Implementation",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Many Parents",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "Java",
                "Python"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Parents",
                  "one `extends`, many `implements`",
                  "many bases, ABCs or Protocols"
                ],
                [
                  "Syntax",
                  "`class Duck implements Flyer, Swimmer`",
                  "`class Duck(Flyer, Swimmer)`"
                ],
                [
                  "Lookup order",
                  "explicit override",
                  "==MRO== (C3 linearization)"
                ]
              ],
              "frame": "dashed"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Diamond Problem",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Java: same `default` method in two interfaces → ==compile error== until you override",
                "Resolve by naming the parent: `Flyer.super.move()`",
                "Python: MRO + `super()` pick the order; check it with `Duck.__mro__`"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "java",
              "lines": [
                "interface Flyer { default void move() {} }",
                "interface Swimmer { default void move() {} }",
                "class Duck implements Flyer, Swimmer {",
                "  public void move() { Flyer.super.move(); }",
                "}"
              ],
              "note": "override is mandatory"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "python",
              "lines": [
                "class Flyer: pass",
                "class Swimmer: pass",
                "class Duck(Flyer, Swimmer): pass",
                "print([c.__name__ for c in Duck.__mro__])"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "output",
              "lines": [
                "['Duck', 'Flyer', 'Swimmer', 'object']"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Why Interfaces Stack",
              "ink": "orange"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "No multiple state",
              "text": "Java forbids multiple inheritance of **state**, so you extend ==one== abstract class. Interfaces carry no state, so one class can hold many **capabilities**."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Default Methods and Choosing Between Them",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 2,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Default Methods and Choosing Between Them",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Default Methods",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Java 8 ==default== methods give an interface a body",
                "Add methods **without breaking** existing implementers",
                "No instance fields; only calls {red:other interface methods}",
                "Python: ABCs hold ==concrete methods==",
                "Protocol default bodies are inherited by explicit subclasses"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "java",
              "lines": [
                "interface Logger {",
                "  void write(String m);",
                "  default void log() { write(\"hi\"); }",
                "}"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Interface or Abstract Class",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "Interface",
                "Abstract class"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Pick when",
                  "Unrelated classes share a capability",
                  "Subclasses share state or constructor logic"
                ],
                [
                  "Per class",
                  "Many",
                  "One"
                ],
                [
                  "Members",
                  "Minimal contract",
                  "Non-public members"
                ],
                [
                  "Flow",
                  "None",
                  "Controls common skeleton"
                ]
              ],
              "frame": "dashed"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Rule",
              "text": "Start with an ==interface==; add an abstract class only to share code."
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Interview Point",
              "ink": "orange"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "**Can a default method hold state?** No, interfaces have no instance fields.",
                "**Why default methods?** Evolve an interface without breaking implementers.",
                "**When an abstract class?** Shared state, constructors, or a fixed skeleton."
              ],
              "style": "check"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Programming to an Interface",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 3,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Programming to an Interface",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Depend on the abstraction",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Declare the ==interface type==, construct the concrete one: `List<String> xs = new ArrayList<>();`",
                "Python: `def run(store: Storage)` takes any {blue:Storage}, not one class",
                "Accept the interface in **parameters, fields and return types**",
                "Implementations swap with ==no change== to callers"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Injection and testing",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "kind": "flow",
              "spec": {
                "nodes": [
                  {
                    "text": "Caller",
                    "sub": "knows Repo only",
                    "ink": "blue"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Repo",
                    "sub": "interface",
                    "ink": "purple"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "DbRepo",
                    "sub": "production",
                    "ink": "green"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "InMemoryRepo",
                    "sub": "unit tests",
                    "ink": "orange"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "==Dependency injection==: pass the implementation in from outside",
                "Unit tests pass a fake `InMemoryRepo` instead of a real `DbRepo`"
              ],
              "style": "check"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Python: Protocol",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`Protocol` is a **structural** contract",
                "Any class with a matching `get` satisfies it, with ==no inheritance=="
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "python",
              "lines": [
                "class Repo(Protocol):",
                "    def get(self, id: int) -> User: ...",
                "",
                "class InMemoryRepo:  # no base class",
                "    def get(self, id: int) -> User:",
                "        return USERS[id]",
                "",
                "def run(store: Repo): ..."
              ],
              "note": "InMemoryRepo satisfies Repo"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "4. Mistake",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "No interface for every class",
              "text": "An interface with ==one implementation== and no variation is noise. Add one when you need a **seam**: a swap or test point."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
